"executive_summary": "This paper introduces BanStereoSet, a novel dataset designed to evaluate stereotypical social biases in Large Language Models (LLMs) specifically for the Bangla language. Recognizing the dominance of English-centric bias research, the authors localized content from existing benchmarks like StereoSet, IndiBias, and GenAssocBias to create a culturally relevant resource covering nine bias categories: race, profession, gender, ageism, beauty, beauty in profession, region, caste, and religion. The dataset comprises 1,194 fill-in-the-blank sentences, meticulously curated and translated using a human-in-the-loop process to address linguistic nuances such as the absence of gendered pronouns in Bangla. Experimental evaluations were conducted on four state-of-the-art multilingual LLMs (GPT-4o, Mistral-7B, Llama3.1-70B, and Gemma2-27B) using both Bangla and English versions of the dataset. Results indicate significant biases across all models, with GPT-4o showing higher stereotypical engagement on average, while Mistral performed best in 7 of the 9 categories. Notably, the study reveals a language-dependent cultural sensitivity, where Mistral successfully handled caste bias in Bangla but not in English. The findings underscore the necessity of linguistically and culturally adapted datasets to develop equitable AI technologies for non-English speaking communities, while also highlighting limitations regarding regional representation and binary gender constructs.",
